    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Given that \\(\\det \\mathbf{M} = -2\\), we want to find \\(\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4)\\).\n\nFirst, recall the property of determinants that states:\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^n) = [\\det \\mathbf{M}]^n\n\\]\nIn this problem, we are given \\(\\mathbf{M}^4\\), so we substitute \\(n = 4\\) into the property:\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = [\\det \\mathbf{M}]^4\n\\]\n\nGiven \\(\\det \\mathbf{M} = -2\\), we can replace \\([\\det \\mathbf{M}]\\) with \\(-2\\):\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = (-2)^4\n\\]\n\nNext, compute \\((-2)^4\\):\n\\[\n(-2)^4 = (-2) \\times (-2) \\times (-2) \\times (-2) = 4 \\times 4 = 16\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = 16\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{16}\n\\]",
