    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given that \\(\\det \\mathbf{M} = -2,\\) we need to find \\(\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4).\\)\n\nFirst, recall the property of determinants for matrix powers:\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^n) = (\\det \\mathbf{M})^n.\n\\]\nIn this case, \\(n = 4,\\) so\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = (\\det \\mathbf{M})^4.\n\\]\nSince \\(\\det \\mathbf{M} = -2,\\) we have\n\\[\n\\det (\\mathbf{M}^4) = (-2)^4.\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\((-2)^4\\):\n\\[\n(-2)^4 = (-2) \\times (-2) \\times (-2) \\times (-2).\n\\]\n\nLet's compute this step by step:\n\\[\n(-2) \\times (-2) = 4,\n\\]\n\\[\n4 \\times (-2) = -8,\n\\]\n\\[\n-8 \\times (-2) = 16.\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\\[\n(-2)^4 = 16.\n\\]\n\nTo conclude,\n\\[\n\\boxed{16}.\n\\]",
